## DESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Simple linear regression)
## DBsection(Regression)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz, L Danielson, J Guild)
## MLT(LineFromData)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('The Basic Practice of Statistics')
## AuthorText1('David Moore')
## EditionText1('5e')
## Section1('5')
## KEYWORDS('statistic', 'regression')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGstatisticsmacros.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

#  Set-up

@x = (
    random(56.1, 57.8, .1),
    random(43.2, 47.9, .1),
    random(40.3, 43.8, .1),
    random(43.2, 48.9, .1),
    random(52.1, 58.7, .1)
);

@y = (
    random(189.4, 193.3, .1),
    random(150.5, 155.7, .1),
    random(143.1, 149.8, .1),
    random(161.1, 166.9, .1),
    random(168.0, 175.7, .1)
);

($b1, $b0) = linear_regression(~~@x, ~~@y);

$f = Compute("$b0 + $b1 x");

$tab = DataTable(
    [
        [ PGML('*Knee Height*'), PGML('[`x`]'), @x ],
        [ PGML('*Height* '),     PGML('[`y`]'), @y ]
    ],
    align           => '|cc' . ('|c' x 5) . '|',
    horizontalrules => 1
);

BEGIN_PGML
Because elderly people may have difficulty standing to have their height measured, a study looked at the relationship between overall height and height to the knee. Here are data (in centimeters) for five elderly men:

[$tab]*

What is the equation of the least-squares regression line for predicting height from knee height?

ANSWER:  [` \hat{y} = `] [_]{$f}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Using software, the slope is [@ Round($b1,6) @] and the intercept is [@ Round($b0,6)@].  The regression line is
[```\hat{y} = [$f]```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
